window.QUIZ_BANK['git-github'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['git-github']||[]).concat([
{"id":3241,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is GitHub?","options":["A standalone Git client application for Windows","A web-based platform that provides hosting for Git repositories along with collaboration features like pull requests, issues, and actions","A database management system that uses Git for version control","An IDE plugin that integrates Git commands into code editors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub is a cloud-based platform built around Git that provides repository hosting plus collaboration tools: pull requests for code review, Issues for bug tracking, Actions for CI/CD automation, Projects for project management, and a social coding community. It was acquired by Microsoft in 2018."},
{"id":3242,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is the difference between a public and private GitHub repository?","options":["Public repos use HTTPS only; private repos use SSH only","Public repos are visible and cloneable by anyone; private repos restrict access to explicitly invited collaborators","Public repos support GitHub Actions while private repos do not","There is no difference — access is controlled at the file level in both cases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A public GitHub repository is visible to anyone on the internet; anyone can clone it and view its full history. A private repository restricts all access to the owner and explicitly invited collaborators. Organizations can set baseline member permissions for private repos within their organization."},
{"id":3243,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is a GitHub fork?","options":["A Git branch with enhanced conflict resolution","A copy of another user's repository placed under your own GitHub account, allowing you to freely experiment and propose changes via pull requests","A type of merge strategy that preserves the commit history","A protected branch that cannot be deleted or force-pushed to"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forking creates a personal copy of someone else's repository on your GitHub account. You can freely modify your fork without affecting the original. When you want to contribute back, you open a pull request from your fork's branch to the upstream repository. This is the standard open-source contribution workflow on GitHub."},
{"id":3244,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is a GitHub Pull Request (PR)?","options":["A request to pull code from a remote to your local machine","A proposal to merge changes from one branch into another, enabling code review, discussion, and automated checks before merging","A feature that automatically pulls all open issues and assigns them to reviewers","A dashboard that shows the status of remote branches"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Pull Request is GitHub's mechanism for proposing changes. It shows the diff between a source branch and target branch, enables inline code review comments, links to CI/CD status checks, and provides a controlled merge interface. PRs are central to modern collaborative Git workflows."},
{"id":3245,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What are the three PR merge strategies available on GitHub?","options":["Fast-forward, squash, and revert","Merge commit, Squash and merge, Rebase and merge","Standard merge, Forced merge, Conflict merge","Linear merge, Parallel merge, Cherry merge"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub offers three merge strategies: (1) 'Create a merge commit' — preserves all commits with a merge commit, (2) 'Squash and merge' — combines all PR commits into a single commit on the target branch, and (3) 'Rebase and merge' — replays PR commits individually on top of the base branch without a merge commit."},
{"id":3246,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What happens when you 'Squash and merge' a Pull Request on GitHub?","options":["All commits in the PR are preserved individually on the base branch","All commits from the PR branch are squashed into a single new commit on the base branch, and the PR branch history is condensed","The PR is merged with a 3-way merge commit and all commits preserved","The PR commits are rebased and replayed maintaining individual authorship"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'Squash and merge' combines all commits from the pull request into one new commit placed on the base branch. This keeps the base branch history clean and linear. However, individual PR commit details are lost from the base branch history — only the squash commit message (typically listing all commits) remains."},
{"id":3247,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'Rebase and merge' do when merging a PR on GitHub?","options":["Creates a merge commit with the base branch as the first parent","Replays each commit from the PR individually on top of the base branch, creating a linear history without a merge commit","Squashes all commits then rebases the single commit","Merges only if the branch is up to date with the base"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'Rebase and merge' takes each commit from the PR branch, rewrites them with new hashes as if they were originally made on top of the current base branch HEAD, then fast-forwards the base branch. The result is a linear history with all PR commits individually visible but no explicit merge commit."},
{"id":3248,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What are GitHub Issues used for?","options":["Tracking down Git binary corruption problems","Reporting bugs, requesting features, asking questions, and tracking work items for a project","Managing GitHub billing and invoices","Storing environment variables for GitHub Actions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Issues are a built-in project tracking system. They can represent bug reports, feature requests, documentation improvements, or general tasks. Issues support labels, assignees, milestones, cross-references, and can be linked to pull requests. They serve as the primary task management system for many open-source and private projects."},
{"id":3249,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you automatically close a GitHub Issue when a Pull Request is merged?","options":["By adding the issue number in the PR branch name","By using keywords like 'Closes #42' or 'Fixes #42' in the PR description or commit message","By manually linking the issue in the issue tracker after merge","GitHub automatically closes all open issues when a PR is merged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub recognizes closing keywords (Closes, Fixes, Resolves) followed by an issue reference (e.g., 'Closes #42') in pull request descriptions or commit messages. When the PR is merged into the default branch, the referenced issue is automatically closed. This links code changes to the tracked issues they address."},
{"id":3250,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a GitHub Project (Projects)?","options":["A GitHub Actions pipeline configuration","A Kanban-style or table-based project management tool integrated with GitHub Issues and Pull Requests","A hosted database for storing project configuration files","A billing unit for GitHub Enterprise organizations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Projects provides a flexible project management interface with views like boards (Kanban), tables (spreadsheet-like), and roadmaps. Cards can be linked to Issues and Pull Requests, automatically updating based on status. Projects v2 introduced custom fields, filtering, and workflow automation."},
{"id":3251,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What file must exist in a GitHub repository to define a GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["A .github/workflows/*.yml file in the repository","A Makefile in the root of the repository","A .actions file in the .git directory","A github-actions.json file in the repository root"],"answer":0,"explanation":"GitHub Actions workflows are defined as YAML files placed in the .github/workflows/ directory of the repository. Each .yml (or .yaml) file in that directory is treated as a separate workflow. The workflow file defines triggers, jobs, and steps that GitHub Actions executes automatically."},
{"id":3252,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"In a GitHub Actions workflow YAML, what does the 'on' key define?","options":["The name of the runner machine to execute the workflow on","The event triggers that cause the workflow to run (e.g., push, pull_request, schedule)","The list of actions to run in sequence","The environment variables available to the workflow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'on' key in a GitHub Actions workflow YAML defines the events that trigger the workflow to run. You can specify GitHub events (push, pull_request, release), scheduled cron-based triggers (schedule), manual triggers (workflow_dispatch), or calls from other workflows (workflow_call)."},
{"id":3253,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What does the 'jobs' key define in a GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["The list of secrets available to the workflow","The individual units of work that run in parallel (or in sequence with needs:), each consisting of a set of steps on a runner","The branches or tags that trigger the workflow","The output artifacts produced by the workflow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'jobs' is the top-level key that defines one or more named job blocks. Each job specifies a runner ('runs-on'), a set of steps to execute, and optionally dependencies on other jobs ('needs'). By default all jobs run in parallel; 'needs' creates sequential dependencies."},
{"id":3254,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'steps' key within a GitHub Actions job?","options":["The number of retry attempts if the job fails","An ordered list of individual tasks within a job — each step is either a shell command ('run') or a pre-built action ('uses')","The list of dependent jobs that must complete first","The configuration for the runner environment setup"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'steps' is an ordered list of operations within a job. Each step can use a pre-built action from the marketplace ('uses: actions/checkout@v4') or run a shell command ('run: npm test'). Steps execute sequentially within a job and share the runner's filesystem and environment."},
{"id":3255,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the 'uses' keyword in a GitHub Actions step specify?","options":["The shell interpreter to use for the run command","A reference to a pre-built action (from the Marketplace or a repository path) to execute as that step","The Docker image to pull for the step","The GitHub App permission level required for the step"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'uses' in a step references a pre-built GitHub Action to run. The format is 'owner/repo@ref' (e.g., 'actions/checkout@v4') for Marketplace actions or './path/to/action' for local actions. Pre-built actions are reusable units that encapsulate complex operations like checking out code or setting up language runtimes."},
{"id":3256,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which GitHub Actions trigger fires when commits are pushed to the repository?","options":["on: commit","on: push","on: sync","on: upload"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'push' event fires a workflow whenever commits are pushed to the repository, including direct pushes and merged pull requests. You can filter by branch patterns ('branches: [main]') or file path patterns ('paths: [src/**]') to run the workflow only for relevant pushes."},
{"id":3257,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the 'workflow_dispatch' trigger enable in GitHub Actions?","options":["Automatic workflow triggering based on deployment events","Manual triggering of a workflow from the GitHub Actions UI or via the GitHub API, optionally accepting user-defined inputs","Triggering workflows based on external webhook events","Cascading workflow execution based on another workflow's completion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'workflow_dispatch' allows a workflow to be triggered manually. Users can click 'Run workflow' in the GitHub Actions UI, or trigger it via the REST API. You can define 'inputs' in the workflow to collect parameters from the user at trigger time, making workflows interactive and reusable for on-demand tasks."},
{"id":3258,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you schedule a GitHub Actions workflow to run every day at midnight UTC?","options":["on: schedule: - cron: '0 0 * * *'","on: timer: daily: midnight","on: schedule: daily","on: cron: '0 0 * * *'"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 'schedule' trigger uses POSIX cron syntax under 'on: schedule: - cron:'. '0 0 * * *' means 'at minute 0 of hour 0 every day'. Note that scheduled workflows only run on the default branch, and GitHub may delay them during high load. The minimum interval is every 5 minutes."},
{"id":3259,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'pull_request' trigger in GitHub Actions?","options":["A trigger that fires when a git pull is run locally","A trigger that fires on pull request events (opened, closed, synchronized, etc.) targeting the repository","A trigger for requesting access to private repositories","A trigger that only fires when the pull request is from a fork"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'pull_request' event triggers workflows on PR lifecycle events. By default it fires on 'opened', 'synchronize' (new commits pushed), and 'reopened'. You can specify additional activity types like 'closed', 'labeled', etc. and filter by target branch with 'branches:'."},
{"id":3260,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What runner label corresponds to the latest available Ubuntu Linux virtual machine on GitHub-hosted runners?","options":["ubuntu","ubuntu-latest","linux-latest","github-ubuntu"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'ubuntu-latest' is the runner label for the latest stable Ubuntu LTS environment on GitHub's hosted infrastructure. Other hosted runner labels include 'windows-latest' (Windows Server) and 'macos-latest' (macOS). GitHub updates the 'latest' label periodically; for reproducibility, you can pin to a specific version like 'ubuntu-22.04'."},
{"id":3261,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you reference a GitHub Actions secret named 'API_KEY' inside a workflow YAML?","options":["$API_KEY","env.API_KEY","${{ secrets.API_KEY }}","secrets['API_KEY']"],"answer":2,"explanation":"GitHub Actions uses the expression syntax '${{ secrets.API_KEY }}' to access secrets stored in the repository or organization settings. Secrets are never printed in logs — GitHub automatically masks their values. They are available in 'env:' blocks or directly in step parameters using this expression syntax."},
{"id":3262,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the difference between a repository secret and an environment secret in GitHub Actions?","options":["They are identical — environment is just an alias for repository","Repository secrets are available to all workflows in the repo; environment secrets are only available to jobs that specify that deployment environment and may require additional approval gates","Environment secrets are more secure and encrypted differently than repository secrets","Repository secrets expire monthly while environment secrets are permanent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Repository secrets are accessible by all jobs in all workflows of the repository. Environment secrets are scoped to a named deployment environment (e.g., 'production') and are only injected into jobs that declare 'environment: production'. Environments can also have required reviewers who must approve before the job accesses those secrets."},
{"id":3263,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What are GitHub Actions artifacts, and how do you upload them?","options":["Pre-built Docker images used by Actions runners","Files produced by a workflow job (builds, test results, logs) that are uploaded with 'actions/upload-artifact' for download or use by subsequent jobs","The marketplace listings for available Actions","Log files automatically created by GitHub during workflow execution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Artifacts are files you want to persist from a job — compiled binaries, test reports, coverage files, logs. Use 'actions/upload-artifact@v4' with a name and path to upload them. Use 'actions/download-artifact@v4' in a subsequent job to retrieve them. Artifacts are retained for 90 days by default (configurable)."},
{"id":3264,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of 'actions/cache' in GitHub Actions?","options":["Caches the results of each step to skip re-execution","Saves and restores directories (like dependency caches) between workflow runs to speed up builds by avoiding re-downloading packages","Caches Docker layers for container builds only","Stores workflow run logs in a faster retrieval system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'actions/cache' saves specified directories (e.g., ~/.m2/repository, node_modules) at the end of a run and restores them at the start of subsequent runs using a cache key. This avoids re-downloading dependencies on every run, significantly speeding up workflows. Cache hits are logged; misses cause a fresh download and new cache save."},
{"id":3265,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the GitHub Actions matrix strategy used for?","options":["Organizing workflow steps in a visual matrix table in the GitHub UI","Running the same job with multiple variable combinations (e.g., different OS, language version, or config) in parallel","Distributing work across multiple repositories simultaneously","Multiplying the runner count to handle more workflow triggers simultaneously"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The matrix strategy allows you to define a set of variables, and GitHub Actions automatically creates a separate parallel job instance for each combination. For example, 'matrix: os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest] node-version: [18, 20]' creates 4 parallel jobs testing across all OS and Node.js version combinations."},
{"id":3266,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you define a matrix strategy in a GitHub Actions job?","options":["uses: matrix-strategy@v1 with the matrix definition","strategy: matrix: with key-value arrays under the job definition","run: matrix: [values]","on: matrix: with parallel job definitions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The matrix is defined under 'strategy: matrix:' within a job. You define variables as arrays (e.g., 'os: [ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]'), and reference them in the job via '${{ matrix.os }}'. Use 'include:' to add extra variable combinations and 'exclude:' to remove specific combinations from the matrix."},
{"id":3267,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is GitHub Pages?","options":["A documentation standard GitHub enforces for all public repositories","A GitHub feature that serves static websites directly from a repository's branch or GitHub Actions workflow","A service for hosting private API documentation behind authentication","A feature for embedding GitHub commit history into external websites"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Pages hosts static websites (HTML, CSS, JS, Jekyll sites) directly from a GitHub repository. You can serve from the main branch root, a /docs folder, or a dedicated gh-pages branch, or publish from a GitHub Actions workflow to the special gh-pages environment. It supports custom domains with HTTPS."},
{"id":3268,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a GitHub Release?","options":["A notification sent to followers when code is pushed","A formal versioned distribution package created from a Git tag, containing release notes and optional binary assets for download","A GitHub feature for scheduling deployment windows","A draft of a PR that is not yet ready for review"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A GitHub Release is a higher-level abstraction built on top of a Git tag. It provides a dedicated page with release notes, a changelog, and downloadable assets (compiled binaries, archives). Users can subscribe to release notifications. Releases can be created manually or automated via GitHub Actions."},
{"id":3269,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'needs' do in a GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["Declares required software dependencies to be installed on the runner","Creates a sequential dependency between jobs — the dependent job only starts after the specified job(s) succeed","Specifies that the workflow needs manual approval before running","Declares that the workflow needs access to specific secrets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'needs: [job-name]' in a job definition creates an explicit dependency on one or more other jobs. The dependent job only runs after all listed jobs complete successfully. This enables sequential pipelines (build → test → deploy) while still allowing unrelated jobs to run in parallel."},
{"id":3270,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you pass outputs from one GitHub Actions job to another?","options":["Outputs are automatically shared between all jobs via shared memory","Use 'outputs:' in the producing job to expose step outputs, and reference them in the consuming job with '${{ needs.<job-id>.outputs.<name> }}'","Write values to a shared artifact file and download it in the next job","GitHub Actions jobs cannot share data — use separate workflows instead"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Job outputs are defined in the job's 'outputs:' block mapping names to step output expressions. Steps export values by writing to '$GITHUB_OUTPUT'. A dependent job accesses them via '${{ needs.<job-id>.outputs.<output-name> }}'. For large data, artifacts are preferred; outputs are best for small scalar values."},
{"id":3271,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'checkout' action (actions/checkout) used for?","options":["It checks out a remote branch onto the runner's filesystem so subsequent steps can access repository code","It validates the syntax of the GitHub Actions YAML workflow file","It authenticates the runner with the GitHub API","It checks the repository for security vulnerabilities"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'actions/checkout' is almost always the first step in a GitHub Actions job. It clones the repository (or the triggering commit/PR branch) onto the runner's filesystem, making the repository code available to all subsequent steps. Without it, subsequent steps cannot access your repository files."},
{"id":3272,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What environment variable contains the path to the GitHub Actions workspace directory?","options":["$GITHUB_WORKSPACE","$RUNNER_WORKSPACE","$ACTIONS_DIR","$REPO_PATH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'$GITHUB_WORKSPACE' is a built-in environment variable that contains the absolute path to the repository directory on the runner where 'actions/checkout' places the repository files. All relative file paths in workflow steps are typically relative to this directory."},
{"id":3273,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'GITHUB_TOKEN' secret in GitHub Actions?","options":["A personal access token the user must create and add as a secret","An automatically provided temporary token with scoped repository permissions that GitHub creates for each workflow run, used for interacting with the GitHub API","A token that grants admin access to the entire GitHub organization","A token used exclusively for pushing to the gh-pages branch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'GITHUB_TOKEN' is a temporary JWT token GitHub automatically generates for each workflow run with permissions scoped to the repository. It can create releases, comment on PRs, push commits, and call GitHub APIs without requiring a personal access token. Permissions can be further restricted in the workflow YAML."},
{"id":3274,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the 'env' key in a GitHub Actions step or job do?","options":["Lists the environment name (production, staging) for deployment","Sets environment variables available to that step or job, which can reference secrets or other expressions","Specifies the runner operating system environment","Configures the Node.js or Python environment version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'env' key sets environment variables at the job level (available to all steps in that job) or at the step level (available only to that step). Values can be literals, expressions referencing secrets ('${{ secrets.TOKEN }}'), or other context values. Step-level env overrides job-level env for the same variable name."},
{"id":3275,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you make a GitHub Actions job run only on the 'main' branch?","options":["branch: main under the job definition","if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' as a job condition","runs-on: main","target-branch: main under strategy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'if:' conditional on a job or step uses GitHub Actions expressions. '${{ github.ref == \"refs/heads/main\" }}' evaluates to true only when the workflow runs on the main branch. This is useful for deploying only from main while running tests on all branches."},
{"id":3276,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the 'continue-on-error: true' setting do in a GitHub Actions step?","options":["Causes the entire workflow to continue even if the runner disconnects","Allows a step to fail without causing the overall job to fail, treating failure as a non-blocking warning","Retries the step up to 3 times before marking it as failed","Continues the step execution even if its timeout is exceeded"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'continue-on-error: true' on a step marks its failure as non-fatal — the job continues to the next step even if this one exits with a non-zero code. The step is still recorded as failed in the UI, but the job overall can still succeed. This is useful for optional steps like generating coverage reports."},
{"id":3277,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is GitHub's default permission level for the GITHUB_TOKEN in a workflow?","options":["Read-only for all repository resources","Read/write for repository contents and packages; read-only for pull requests","Full admin access to the repository and organization","Write access to secrets and environment variables only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By default, the GITHUB_TOKEN has read/write access to repository contents, packages, and deployments. However, the default can be configured per organization or repository to 'read-only' for all scopes. Individual workflows can explicitly declare granular permissions with the 'permissions:' key to follow the principle of least privilege."},
{"id":3278,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'actions/setup-node' do in a GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["Installs the GitHub CLI on the runner","Configures a specific Node.js version on the runner and optionally caches npm/yarn/pnpm dependencies","Creates a Node.js project scaffold in the workspace","Validates the package.json file for correct syntax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'actions/setup-node' sets up the specified Node.js version on the runner (downloading it if necessary), adds node and npm to PATH, and can configure package manager caching. It is the standard way to ensure a consistent, specific Node.js version for build and test steps in JavaScript/TypeScript workflows."},
{"id":3279,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What GitHub Actions context provides information about the triggering event and repository?","options":["workflow context","event context","github context","runner context"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The 'github' context is a built-in context providing rich metadata about the current workflow run and triggering event. Key properties include: github.event_name, github.ref (branch/tag), github.sha (commit hash), github.actor (user who triggered it), github.repository, and github.event (full webhook payload)."},
{"id":3280,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you specify that a GitHub Actions job requires manual approval before running?","options":["Add 'approval: required' to the job definition","Use a deployment environment with required reviewers configured — the job waits for a reviewer to approve before proceeding","Add 'manual: true' under the job's trigger conditions","Create a separate workflow_dispatch trigger that calls the job"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deployment environments in GitHub can be configured with required reviewers. When a job references such an environment ('environment: production'), GitHub pauses execution at that job and sends notifications to the required reviewers. The job only proceeds after approval, enabling protected deployment gates."},
{"id":3281,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'runs-on: self-hosted' in a GitHub Actions job mean?","options":["The job runs inside a Docker container on GitHub's servers","The job runs on a self-hosted runner machine that you manage and have registered with your GitHub repository or organization","The job runs locally on the developer's machine","The job runs on a GitHub-hosted runner with self-managed software"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'self-hosted' targets custom runner machines that you install, maintain, and register with GitHub. Self-hosted runners can run on your own servers, VMs, containers, or cloud instances. They are useful for specialized hardware, private network access, compliance requirements, or when GitHub-hosted runner specs are insufficient."},
{"id":3282,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the 'with' key in a GitHub Actions step using 'uses'?","options":["Specifies additional files to copy alongside the action","Passes input parameters to the action being used, configuring its behavior","Specifies which runner environment to use for that specific step","Declares the permissions required for the action"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'with:' provides named input parameters to a GitHub Action referenced by 'uses:'. These inputs are defined in the action's action.yml file. For example, 'actions/checkout' accepts 'ref:' to checkout a specific branch/tag, 'fetch-depth:' for shallow/deep clones, and 'token:' for authentication."},
{"id":3283,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you cache npm dependencies in a GitHub Actions workflow using actions/cache?","options":["uses: actions/cache@v4 with key based on package-lock.json hash and path set to node_modules","uses: actions/npm-cache@v1 with auto-detect","run: npm ci --cache automatically caches without any action","uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with cache: 'npm' (handles caching internally)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The modern approach is 'actions/setup-node@v4' with 'cache: npm' (or 'yarn', 'pnpm'). This handles the full caching lifecycle automatically using the lock file as the cache key. Alternatively, 'actions/cache@v4' can be used manually with a key like '${{ hashFiles(\"**/package-lock.json\") }}' and path '~/.npm'."},
{"id":3284,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of 'actions/upload-artifact' with 'retention-days'?","options":["Sets how many days the artifact is available for download before GitHub automatically deletes it","Sets how many days old an artifact must be before it can be uploaded","Limits the upload to artifacts no older than the specified days","Sets the retention period for the workflow run log, not the artifact"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'retention-days' in 'actions/upload-artifact' sets the artifact lifetime. The default is 90 days (or as configured in organization/repository settings). Setting a shorter value (e.g., retention-days: 7) for large artifacts like build outputs helps manage GitHub storage usage and costs."},
{"id":3285,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the 'fail-fast' setting do in a GitHub Actions matrix?","options":["Fails the workflow immediately if any step takes longer than expected","When true (default), cancels all other in-progress matrix jobs as soon as any one matrix job fails","Makes each matrix job fail faster by increasing the job timeout","Raises an error if the matrix has no matching combinations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'strategy: fail-fast: true' (the default) means that if any job in a matrix fails, GitHub Actions immediately cancels all other in-progress matrix jobs. Setting 'fail-fast: false' lets all matrix combinations run to completion even when some fail, which is useful for seeing the full scope of failures across all combinations."},
{"id":3286,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What GitHub event triggers a workflow when a new GitHub Release is published?","options":["on: deploy","on: release: types: [published]","on: tag: created","on: release: created"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'release' event with activity type 'published' triggers when a release is published (not just created as a draft). Other release types include 'created', 'edited', 'deleted', 'prereleased', 'released'. This is commonly used to trigger automated asset building, publishing to package registries, or container image builds."},
{"id":3287,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you add a label to a GitHub Actions runner for targeted job routing?","options":["by adding labels: [custom-label] inside the workflow YAML","By adding custom labels when registering the self-hosted runner, then referencing those labels in runs-on: [self-hosted, custom-label]","By configuring runner groups in organization settings","Labels are only supported for GitHub-hosted runners"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When registering a self-hosted runner, you add custom labels. Jobs then target specific runners by listing those labels in 'runs-on:' as an array, e.g., 'runs-on: [self-hosted, linux, gpu]'. All listed labels must match a single runner. This routes jobs to runners with specific capabilities like GPU, specialized software, or private network access."},
{"id":3288,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'github.event.pull_request.head.sha' expression used for in a workflow?","options":["It refers to the commit SHA of the base branch before the PR is merged","It refers to the commit SHA of the latest commit on the PR's source branch, useful for checkout and status checks","It is the SHA of the merge commit that will be created after merge","It refers to the SHA of the first commit in the PR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'github.event.pull_request.head.sha' is the commit SHA of the HEAD of the PR source branch. It is often used to checkout the exact PR commit for testing ('actions/checkout' with 'ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}') or to post commit status checks to the correct commit."},
{"id":3289,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What is the difference between 'github.sha' and 'github.event.pull_request.head.sha' in a pull_request-triggered workflow?","options":["They are always identical in pull_request triggered workflows","github.sha is the merge commit SHA (what the merged result would look like) while github.event.pull_request.head.sha is the actual latest commit on the PR branch","github.sha is the base branch SHA while event.head.sha is the PR branch SHA","github.sha is undefined for pull_request triggers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"On pull_request events, GitHub merges the PR head into the base branch in a temporary ref for testing. 'github.sha' is the SHA of this temporary merge commit, while 'github.event.pull_request.head.sha' is the actual commit on the PR branch. For checkout of the actual PR code, use head.sha; for testing merged results, use github.sha."},
{"id":3290,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is GitHub Packages?","options":["A marketplace for selling GitHub Actions to other developers","A package registry integrated into GitHub for hosting npm, Maven, NuGet, Docker, RubyGems, and other package types alongside the source code","A feature for managing .gitignore templates and package configuration files","A billing tier for GitHub organizations with large storage needs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Packages is a package hosting service supporting npm, Apache Maven, NuGet, RubyGems, Docker, and Gradle package formats. Packages are scoped to repositories or organizations, supporting private packages for paid plans. It integrates seamlessly with GITHUB_TOKEN for authentication in GitHub Actions workflows."},
{"id":3291,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you trigger a workflow in a downstream repository from a GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["Use on: trigger: downstream in the downstream workflow","Use 'repository_dispatch' event: send a POST request to the GitHub API to trigger the target repo's repository_dispatch event","Link workflows using uses: owner/repo/.github/workflows/file.yml directly","Use the 'trigger' keyword with the target repository path"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'repository_dispatch' allows external triggers via GitHub API (POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/dispatches with a client_payload). This is used for cross-repository workflow triggers, such as a CI workflow in a library repository triggering integration tests in a consuming application repository."},
{"id":3292,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What is the purpose of 'concurrency' groups in GitHub Actions?","options":["Controls how many matrix jobs can run simultaneously","Prevents multiple workflow runs from executing simultaneously for the same context (e.g., same branch), optionally cancelling in-progress runs when a new one starts","Limits the concurrency of self-hosted runner registrations","Controls the parallel execution within a single job's steps"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'concurrency: group:' defines a named group; only one workflow run with the same group name can proceed at a time. 'cancel-in-progress: true' cancels any running workflow in that group when a new one starts. This prevents queue buildup for fast-moving branches and ensures only the latest commit is being tested."},
{"id":3293,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'actions/github-script' allow you to do?","options":["Embed JavaScript source code in GitHub Issues as live executable scripts","Run JavaScript code in a workflow step with full access to the GitHub API via the octokit client and github context object","Generate GitHub Actions YAML dynamically using a scripting interface","Execute shell scripts with elevated administrator permissions on the runner"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'actions/github-script' lets you write inline JavaScript in a workflow step with pre-configured access to the GitHub API via the '@octokit/rest' client as 'github', the workflow context as 'context', and 'core' for output/logging. It eliminates the need for a full custom action for simple API operations like commenting on PRs or creating issues."},
{"id":3294,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a GitHub Actions environment variable vs. a secret?","options":["They are identical but stored in different locations","Environment variables store non-sensitive config data visible in logs; secrets are encrypted, masked in logs, and intended for sensitive credentials","Environment variables are only available during the job setup phase; secrets are available throughout the job","Environment variables are set by GitHub automatically; secrets are set by the workflow YAML author"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Environment variables ('env:') hold non-sensitive values and are visible in workflow logs. Secrets are encrypted at rest, masked in logs (GitHub replaces the value with ***), and accessible only to authorized jobs. Use secrets for tokens, passwords, and API keys; use environment variables for configuration values like URLs and feature flags."},
{"id":3295,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'on: push: paths-ignore: [\"**.md\"]' do in a GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["Ignores all workflow runs triggered by non-markdown file changes","Prevents the workflow from triggering when the only changed files match the specified patterns (e.g., documentation-only changes)","Marks markdown files as ignored in the git repository","Excludes markdown files from being checked out by actions/checkout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'paths-ignore:' under a push or pull_request trigger tells GitHub Actions not to run the workflow if ALL changed files match the specified patterns. This avoids triggering expensive build/test workflows for documentation-only changes. Use 'paths:' to restrict triggers to specific paths instead."},
{"id":3296,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the GitHub Actions 'timeout-minutes' setting?","options":["The maximum time a workflow can be queued before being cancelled","The maximum duration a job or step is allowed to run before GitHub automatically cancels it","The timeout for connecting to the GitHub API within a step","The time after which unused artifacts are deleted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'timeout-minutes' can be set at the job level (maximum total job duration) or step level (maximum step duration). The default job timeout is 360 minutes (6 hours). Setting explicit, tighter timeouts prevents runaway jobs from consuming unlimited Actions minutes and helps detect hung processes quickly."},
{"id":3297,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of GitHub Actions 'outputs' at the job level?","options":["Defines the files the job writes to the runner filesystem","Exposes named values from the job that dependent jobs can read using the needs context","Controls what artifact files are uploaded to GitHub storage","Sets the exit code output for the entire workflow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Job-level 'outputs:' maps names to step output expressions (e.g., 'version: ${{ steps.get-version.outputs.version }}'). Steps write to outputs by appending 'name=value' to '$GITHUB_OUTPUT'. Downstream jobs that list this job in 'needs:' can then access 'needs.<job>.outputs.<name>'. This is the typed, structured alternative to artifact files for small values."},
{"id":3298,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the GitHub Actions 'runner' context?","options":["A context listing all registered runners in the organization","A context providing metadata about the current runner executing the job: OS, temp directory, workspace, runner name and labels","A context for managing self-hosted runner registration","A context that provides container runtime information for Docker-based jobs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'runner' context exposes properties of the executing runner: 'runner.os' (Linux/Windows/macOS), 'runner.arch' (X64/ARM64), 'runner.name' (runner name), 'runner.temp' (temporary directory path), and 'runner.workspace' (workspace directory). These are useful for writing OS-conditional steps or constructing platform-specific paths."},
{"id":3299,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"advanced","question":"How do you set up a GitHub Actions workflow to build and push a Docker image to Docker Hub?","options":["Use the docker/push action with credentials directly in the YAML","Login using docker/login-action with Docker Hub credentials from secrets, build with docker/build-push-action, then push to Docker Hub","Use git push with a Dockerfile and GitHub auto-builds the image","Use the GITHUB_TOKEN to authenticate directly with Docker Hub"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard workflow uses: (1) 'docker/login-action' with secrets DOCKERHUB_USERNAME and DOCKERHUB_TOKEN to authenticate, (2) 'docker/build-push-action' with 'push: true' and 'tags' to build and push the image. Docker's official GitHub Actions provide build caching, multi-platform builds (buildx), and provenance metadata."},
{"id":3300,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the 'on: workflow_call' trigger enable?","options":["Allows a workflow to call itself recursively","Makes the workflow callable as a reusable workflow from other workflows in the same or other repositories","Triggers when any workflow in the repository completes","Allows the workflow to call external APIs directly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'on: workflow_call' marks a workflow as a reusable workflow that other workflows can invoke using 'uses: owner/repo/.github/workflows/reusable.yml@main'. You can define 'inputs:' and 'secrets:' for the reusable workflow to accept parameters. This enables DRY principles across multiple workflows or repositories."},
{"id":3301,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of GitHub Labels on Issues and Pull Requests?","options":["Encrypted tags that control access to repository contents","Colored text tags for categorizing and filtering Issues and PRs (e.g., 'bug', 'enhancement', 'priority:high'), enabling organized project management","Labels that assign work to specific team members or bots","Metadata fields that GitHub requires for compliance reporting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Labels are customizable, colored text tags that can be applied to Issues and PRs to categorize them by type (bug, enhancement), priority (high, medium), status (in-progress, blocked), component, or any other dimension. Labels make it easy to filter Issues, set up automation via GitHub Actions, and manage work across a repository."},
{"id":3302,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is GitHub Milestones, and how does it relate to Issues?","options":["A GitHub Actions feature for tracking deployment milestone events","A grouping mechanism that associates Issues and PRs with a target version or sprint, tracking progress toward a goal with a progress bar","A type of label that marks issues as high priority milestones","A system that creates automatic release notes from closed issues"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Milestones group Issues and PRs together with a title, description, and optional due date. GitHub shows a progress bar based on the percentage of closed vs. open items in the milestone. Milestones are useful for tracking what needs to be done for a specific version release or sprint."},
{"id":3303,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you request a specific person to review a GitHub Pull Request?","options":["Tag them with @mention in the PR body only","Assign them as a reviewer in the 'Reviewers' sidebar on the PR page, or via the GitHub API or CLI","Open an Issue and link it to the PR requesting review","Create a separate branch called 'review-request/username'"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reviewers are requested via the 'Reviewers' sidebar on the PR page, the GitHub API, or 'gh pr edit --reviewer'. Requested reviewers receive a notification and appear in the PR's review status. CODEOWNERS rules can auto-request reviews from code owners when their owned files are changed."},
{"id":3304,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of a 'Draft Pull Request' on GitHub?","options":["A PR that can only be viewed by repository owners","A PR marked as not yet ready for review — it cannot be merged, CI may still run, and reviewers are signaled not to do a final review yet","A PR automatically created from a draft commit","A PR created from a forked repository before the author has push access"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Draft PRs are explicitly marked as work-in-progress and cannot be merged until converted to 'Ready for review'. They are ideal for early sharing of work for feedback, for CI to run on in-progress code, and to prevent accidental merging. GitHub shows a visual indicator and CI status checks still run normally."},
{"id":3305,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'Require status checks to pass before merging' in GitHub branch protection rules enforce?","options":["It requires all Issues linked to PRs to be closed before merging","It blocks merging a PR if any required CI/CD checks (defined in branch protection settings) have not passed successfully","It requires that the branch is up to date with the base branch","It requires a minimum number of approving reviews from code owners"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Required status checks are external services or GitHub Actions workflows that must report success before a PR can be merged. You specify which check names are required in the branch protection rule settings. This ensures code only merges when it passes tests, linting, security scans, or any other automated verification."},
{"id":3306,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the difference between 'Assignees' and 'Reviewers' on a GitHub Pull Request?","options":["They are the same — both refer to people who must approve the PR","Assignees are people responsible for the PR (often the author or DRI); Reviewers are specifically requested to give code review feedback and approval","Assignees can merge the PR; Reviewers can only comment","Reviewers are from outside the organization; Assignees are from within"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'Assignees' indicate who is responsible for the PR or issue — typically the author or the person driving it to completion. 'Reviewers' are people specifically asked to review the code and provide approval. The distinction is between ownership/accountability (assignee) and review responsibility (reviewer)."},
{"id":3307,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'actions/download-artifact' do in a multi-job GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["Downloads external binary dependencies from the internet","Downloads artifacts uploaded by a previous job in the same workflow run, making the files available in the current job","Downloads the repository source code similar to actions/checkout","Downloads action YAML files from the GitHub Marketplace"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'actions/download-artifact' retrieves artifacts uploaded by 'actions/upload-artifact' in a previous job of the same workflow run. Since jobs run on separate (ephemeral) runners with no shared filesystem, artifacts are the standard way to pass build outputs between jobs — e.g., build job uploads binary, test job downloads and runs it."},
{"id":3308,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'github.workspace' context value in GitHub Actions?","options":["The GitHub organization URL","The absolute path to the workspace directory where actions/checkout places repository files, identical to $GITHUB_WORKSPACE","The name of the repository with owner prefix","The URL of the GitHub Actions workflow run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'${{ github.workspace }}' (context) or '$GITHUB_WORKSPACE' (environment variable) both reference the same path — the root directory where the repository is checked out on the runner. Steps can use this path to reference files in the repository regardless of the runner's working directory."},
{"id":3309,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you check out a Pull Request's code in a GitHub Actions workflow?","options":["uses: actions/checkout@v4 with ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}","uses: actions/checkout@v4 — it automatically detects and checks out PR code by default","uses: actions/pr-checkout@v1","run: git fetch origin pull/${{ github.event.number }}/head:pr-branch && git checkout pr-branch"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For pull_request events, 'actions/checkout@v4' by default checks out the PR's merge commit (the simulated merge of PR into base). To check out the actual PR branch head, use 'ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}'. For PRs from forks, both approaches require careful security consideration to avoid secret exposure."},
{"id":3310,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of GitHub Actions 'permissions' at the workflow or job level?","options":["Grants users GitHub organizational permissions","Restricts or expands the GITHUB_TOKEN permissions for the workflow or job, following the principle of least privilege","Controls filesystem permissions on the runner","Declares which GitHub Apps the workflow can interact with"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'permissions:' key in a workflow or job overrides the GITHUB_TOKEN's default permissions. You can set individual scopes like 'contents: read', 'pull-requests: write', 'issues: write'. Setting explicit minimal permissions is a security best practice, limiting what a compromised workflow step could do with the automatically provisioned token."},
{"id":3311,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What GitHub Actions keyword is used to skip a workflow run for a specific push?","options":["Add [skip ci] or [no ci] anywhere in the commit message","Add //skip-workflow: true to the commit","Push with git push --no-trigger flag","Add skip: true to the workflow YAML file"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Including '[skip ci]', '[ci skip]', '[no ci]', '[skip actions]', or '[actions skip]' in the commit message or PR title tells GitHub to skip triggering all GitHub Actions workflows for that push. This is useful for documentation commits, version bumps, or automated commits that shouldn't trigger a full CI pipeline."},
{"id":3312,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the 'branches' filter under an 'on: push' trigger?","options":["Creates new branches when the workflow runs","Limits the workflow to only trigger on pushes to branches matching the specified name patterns","Lists branches that are protected from direct pushes","Configures which branches the workflow can push commits to"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'on: push: branches: [main, release/**]' restricts the push trigger to only activate for pushes to matching branches. Without this filter, every push to any branch triggers the workflow. You can use glob patterns ('release/**' matches all branches starting with 'release/') and 'branches-ignore:' for exclusion patterns."},
{"id":3313,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What is the security risk of using 'pull_request_target' instead of 'pull_request' in GitHub Actions?","options":["pull_request_target uses more runner minutes than pull_request","pull_request_target runs in the context of the BASE repository (with full secrets) even for PRs from forks, creating a code injection risk if PR code is checked out and executed","pull_request_target is deprecated and may cause workflow failures","pull_request_target cannot access the PR diff or changed files"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'pull_request_target' runs the workflow in the context of the base branch (not the PR branch) and has access to secrets, even for fork PRs. This is designed for safe operations like labeling PRs. The danger: if you checkout and run PR code (from untrusted forks) in a pull_request_target workflow, that code runs with access to secrets — a serious supply-chain vulnerability."},
{"id":3314,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you define required inputs for a 'workflow_dispatch' trigger?","options":["required-inputs: [name, version] as a top-level key","Under 'on: workflow_dispatch: inputs:', define each input with type, description, and required: true","Use env: REQUIRED_INPUT: '' to declare mandatory env vars","Add inputs to the workflow's readme — GitHub reads them automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under 'on: workflow_dispatch: inputs:', you define named inputs with 'type' (string, boolean, choice, environment), 'description', 'required: true/false', and 'default'. Users see these as form fields in the Actions UI. Access them in the workflow via '${{ inputs.input-name }}'."},
{"id":3315,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does 'hashFiles()' do in a GitHub Actions cache key?","options":["Creates a cryptographic signature for the repository","Returns a hash of specified files' contents, commonly used to create a cache key that changes when dependency lock files are updated","Counts the number of files matching a glob pattern","Validates that all listed files exist on the runner filesystem"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'hashFiles(\"**/package-lock.json\")' computes a hash of the matched files' contents. Using this in a cache key (e.g., '${{ runner.os }}-node-${{ hashFiles(\"**/package-lock.json\") }}') ensures the cache is invalidated and refreshed whenever the lock file changes, keeping cached dependencies accurate."},
{"id":3316,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you make a GitHub Actions step run only when a previous step fails?","options":["Add if: failure() to the step","Add on-failure: true to the step","Add catch: true to the step","Add continue-on-error: true to the failing step and add a dependency check"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'if: ${{ failure() }}' on a step makes it conditional on the failure of a previous step in the same job. GitHub Actions provides status check functions: 'success()' (default), 'failure()', 'cancelled()', and 'always()'. These let you define cleanup, notification, or diagnostic steps that run on specific job outcomes."},
{"id":3317,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a GitHub Actions 'composite action'?","options":["An action that builds Docker containers automatically","A type of action defined in an action.yml file using multiple 'runs: using: composite' steps, allowing you to bundle multiple shell commands or action calls into a single reusable action","A matrix strategy that composites multiple jobs together","A workflow that composes multiple reusable workflows in sequence"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Composite actions (action.yml with 'using: composite') let you combine multiple shell commands and 'uses:' action calls into a single reusable action unit. Unlike reusable workflows (which are full jobs), composite actions run within a calling job's steps, sharing the job's runner environment."},
{"id":3318,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What are GitHub Actions 'environments' used for beyond storing secrets?","options":["They are only for secret storage — there are no additional features","They support deployment protection rules: required reviewers who must approve before the job runs, wait timers, and allowed branch restrictions for deployment","They control which Docker images can be used in the environment","They manage GitHub Pages deployment settings for each environment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Environments offer: (1) required reviewers — designated people who must approve before a job using the environment proceeds, (2) wait timers — a delay before the job proceeds after approval, (3) deployment branch rules — only branches/tags matching specified patterns can deploy to the environment. These controls make environments a robust deployment gate system."},
{"id":3319,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of 'actions/checkout' with 'fetch-depth: 0'?","options":["Checks out only the 0th commit (the initial commit)","Fetches the complete git history (all commits and branches) instead of a shallow single-commit clone, required for operations like git log, git blame, or semantic versioning tools that analyze history depth","Creates a detached HEAD at the repository root","Disables LFS file downloading during checkout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By default, 'actions/checkout' does a shallow clone (fetch-depth: 1) — only the latest commit. 'fetch-depth: 0' fetches the full git history including all branches and tags. This is required for tools that analyze commit history (like semantic-release, gitversion, changelog generators) or for accurate 'git log --all' usage."},
{"id":3320,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the GitHub Actions 'steps' context?","options":["A context that lists all available steps in the Marketplace","A context that provides outputs and outcome (success/failure/skipped/cancelled) of previously executed named steps in the current job","A context containing the runner's step-by-step execution log","A context for accessing the workflow YAML definition at runtime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'steps' context allows accessing outputs and outcomes of previously run steps. If a step has 'id: my-step', you can reference 'steps.my-step.outputs.value' for its outputs or 'steps.my-step.outcome' (success/failure/skipped/cancelled) to make subsequent steps conditional on earlier step results."},
{"id":3321,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which GitHub Actions trigger fires when a repository is starred?","options":["on: star","on: watch: types: [started]","on: follow","on: star: types: [created]"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'watch' event with type 'started' fires when a user stars the repository (the 'watch' event was originally named for the starring action in GitHub's API). This can be used for novelty automations but is rarely used in production CI/CD workflows."},
{"id":3322,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'GITHUB_OUTPUT' environment file in GitHub Actions?","options":["A file that contains the complete output log of the workflow run","A special file path GitHub provides; steps append 'name=value' lines to it to set step outputs readable by later steps via the steps context","A file automatically sent to GitHub for workflow debugging","A JSON file containing all available GitHub context variables"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To set a step output, a step appends 'echo \"name=value\" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT' (Linux) or 'echo \"name=value\" >> $env:GITHUB_OUTPUT' (Windows). This replaces the deprecated 'set-output' command. The value is then accessible in subsequent steps as '${{ steps.<step-id>.outputs.name }}'."},
{"id":3323,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you pass secrets into a called reusable workflow?","options":["Reusable workflows automatically inherit all secrets from the caller","Under 'with:' in the calling step, add secrets as regular inputs","Use 'secrets: inherit' to pass all secrets or define named secrets under 'secrets:' in the workflow_call job definition","Secrets cannot be passed to reusable workflows for security reasons"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In the calling workflow's job using a reusable workflow, 'secrets: inherit' passes all the caller's secrets to the reusable workflow. Alternatively, you define specific secrets under 'secrets:' matching the reusable workflow's 'on: workflow_call: secrets:' declarations, then pass them explicitly under 'secrets:' in the calling job."},
{"id":3324,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the GitHub branch protection setting 'Require branches to be up to date before merging' enforce?","options":["The branch must have at least one commit newer than the base branch","The PR branch must be merged/rebased with the latest base branch commits before it can be merged, ensuring CI runs on the most current combined state","The base branch must not have any new commits since the PR was opened","The PR title must reference an up-to-date issue number"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When enabled, this setting prevents merging a PR if the base branch has moved forward with new commits since the PR branch was last updated. This ensures status checks always run on the merged state (PR changes + latest base), preventing stale CI passes from allowing problematic code to merge."},
{"id":3325,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What GitHub feature allows automated dependency version updates via pull requests?","options":["GitHub Renovate","GitHub Dependabot version updates","GitHub AutoMerge","GitHub Package Alerts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Dependabot creates automated PRs to update dependency versions in package files (npm, pip, Maven, Cargo, etc.) when newer versions are available. Configured via .github/dependabot.yml, it schedules regular checks and opens PRs with changelogs. Dependabot security updates are a separate feature that targets known vulnerabilities."},
{"id":3326,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of a GitHub Actions 'service container'?","options":["A container that hosts the GitHub Actions runner process itself","Auxiliary Docker containers (databases, caches, APIs) that spin up alongside the job to provide services for tests without requiring external infrastructure","A container image stored in GitHub Packages for use in workflows","A container that provides a sandboxed execution environment for untrusted steps"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Service containers are additional Docker containers that start alongside a job's runner. Defined under 'services:' in a job, they provide dependencies like PostgreSQL, Redis, or mock APIs needed for integration tests. They are network-accessible by the job steps using their service name as hostname or via mapped ports on localhost."},
{"id":3327,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the default behavior of GitHub Actions when a workflow YAML contains a syntax error?","options":["The workflow runs but skips the invalid steps","GitHub sends the workflow to a fallback safe mode","GitHub rejects the workflow run and reports a parsing error — no jobs are executed","GitHub runs only the valid jobs and skips jobs with syntax errors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If a GitHub Actions workflow YAML file has a syntax error or invalid schema, GitHub cannot parse the workflow and reports an error without executing any jobs. The workflow run is marked as failed immediately at the workflow level. This protects against partial or undefined behavior from malformed workflow definitions."},
{"id":3328,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you specify that a GitHub Actions job should run inside a Docker container instead of directly on the runner VM?","options":["uses: docker://image:tag as the first step","runs-on: docker:image as the runner label","Add a 'container: image:' field to the job definition","Add docker: image: in the strategy block"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adding 'container: image: <image-tag>' to a job definition makes all steps in that job run inside the specified Docker container rather than directly on the VM. You can also specify 'options:', 'env:', 'ports:', and 'volumes:'. This is useful for ensuring a consistent, reproducible build environment."},
{"id":3329,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the GitHub Actions marketplace?","options":["A paid subscription tier for GitHub organizations needing advanced Actions features","A searchable directory of community-built and official GitHub Actions that you can reference with 'uses:' in your workflows","A gallery of GitHub repository templates for common project types","A list of GitHub-approved self-hosted runner providers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The GitHub Actions Marketplace (github.com/marketplace?type=actions) is a searchable catalog of reusable Actions published by the community and vendors. Actions are versioned and referenced by owner/repo@ref. Popular categories include language setup, cloud deployment, testing, notification, security scanning, and CI utilities."},
{"id":3330,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the GitHub organization's Actions settings 'Allow select actions and reusable workflows' enforce?","options":["Only allows paid commercial actions to be used","Restricts which actions can be used in organization workflows — administrators can limit usage to GitHub-owned actions, verified creators, and/or a custom allowlist","Prevents organization members from creating new workflows","Limits the number of concurrent workflow runs across the organization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub organization admins can configure Actions permissions to control which actions are allowed: GitHub-created actions only, GitHub-verified creators, actions in organization repositories, or a custom allowlist. This supply-chain security control prevents use of arbitrary untrusted third-party actions that could exfiltrate secrets or inject malicious code."},
{"id":3331,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the 'jobs.<job-id>.if' conditional used for in GitHub Actions?","options":["To define the job's execution environment based on a condition","To conditionally run or skip an entire job based on an expression evaluated before the job starts","To handle if-else branching within a job's steps","To make job dependencies conditional on specific output values"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'if:' at the job level evaluates an expression and skips the entire job if it evaluates to false. Unlike step-level 'if:', a skipped job shows as 'skipped' in the UI rather than being absent. Common patterns include 'if: github.ref == \"refs/heads/main\"' to only deploy from main."},
{"id":3332,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How can a GitHub Actions workflow be triggered by a comment on a Pull Request?","options":["on: pull_request_comment: types: [created]","on: issue_comment: types: [created] (PR comments are issues in GitHub's API model)","on: pr_comment","on: comment: pull_request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub's API treats PR comments as issue comments. The 'issue_comment' event with type 'created' fires for new comments on both Issues and PRs. Use 'github.event.issue.pull_request' to check if the comment is on a PR. This pattern is used by ChatOps bots that respond to commands like '/deploy' or '/approve' in PR comments."},
{"id":3333,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What security principle should be applied when a GitHub Actions workflow processes inputs from a pull_request event?","options":["Trust all inputs since GitHub validates them before passing to the workflow","Treat all event-sourced inputs as potentially untrusted — never use them directly in 'run:' shell scripts; use intermediate environment variables to prevent script injection attacks","Only workflows from organization members receive potentially malicious inputs","All inputs are automatically sanitized by the GitHub Actions runner before execution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Event-supplied values like PR titles, body text, or branch names can contain shell metacharacters if used in 'run:' commands via direct expression interpolation ('${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}'). Attackers can craft PR titles with shell commands. Always pass values through intermediate 'env:' variables and reference '$ENV_VAR' in the script instead of '${{ }}' directly."},
{"id":3334,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the GitHub Actions 'job summary' feature allow?","options":["A summary email sent to the repository owner after each workflow run","The ability to write formatted markdown to a special file ($GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY) that appears as a rich summary panel on the workflow run page","A compressed log file generated at the end of each job for long-term storage","An auto-generated PR description based on the job's test results"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY' is a file path; writing markdown to it with 'echo \"# Result\" >> $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY' adds rich formatted content to the workflow run summary page. This is used to display test results, coverage reports, deployment summaries, or any useful per-run data without requiring external dashboards."},
{"id":3335,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the '.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md' file?","options":["An automated script that creates pull requests on a schedule","A markdown template that pre-populates the PR description when a new pull request is created, guiding authors to provide consistent information","A file that defines required reviewers for all PRs","A configuration file for GitHub's PR merge settings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md' (in .github/ or the root) is a markdown template pre-loaded into new PR description textboxes. It can include checklists (tests written, documentation updated), context sections (what changed, why, how to test), and links to issue trackers. Multiple templates are supported via .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE/ directory."},
{"id":3336,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does '.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/' support?","options":["Only one template for all issue types","Multiple YAML or markdown issue template files, each providing a different structured form for reporting bugs, requesting features, or other issue types","Automated issue creation from CI failures","Issue templates defined in JSON for external tool integration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/ directory can contain multiple template files (YAML with 'name:', 'labels:', and 'body:' fields for form templates, or markdown with YAML front-matter). Users see a template picker when creating issues. YAML form templates provide structured forms with dropdowns, checkboxes, text inputs, and required fields."},
{"id":3337,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the 'auto-cancel' behavior do for GitHub Actions workflow runs on the same branch?","options":["Automatically cancels all workflow runs older than 24 hours","When a new push to a branch triggers a workflow, the previous in-progress run for the same branch is automatically cancelled","GitHub never auto-cancels runs — they always complete","Runs are auto-cancelled after exceeding the account's free Actions minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By default for push-triggered workflows, GitHub automatically cancels older in-progress workflow runs on the same branch when new commits are pushed. This prevents resource waste on outdated commit builds. The 'concurrency:' key provides explicit, fine-grained control over this cancellation behavior."},
{"id":3338,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a GitHub 'Codespace'?","options":["A branch naming convention for code review environments","A cloud-hosted development environment (VS Code in the browser or remote) pre-configured with a repository's devcontainer.json, providing instant ready-to-code environments","A GitHub Actions runner with a persistent filesystem for ongoing CI work","A GitHub feature for storing code snippets and gists"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GitHub Codespaces provides cloud-hosted development environments based on Visual Studio Code. Each codespace is a container configured via .devcontainer/devcontainer.json with specified tools, extensions, and settings. Developers get a fully configured environment instantly without local setup, accessible from the browser or VS Code desktop."},
{"id":3339,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What GitHub feature provides a 'star' equivalent that developers use to save and organize repositories they find interesting?","options":["GitHub Bookmarks","GitHub Stars — the star button on any repository saves it to the user's 'Starred repositories' list, used as a bookmark and popularity metric","GitHub Favorites","GitHub Collections"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The GitHub Stars feature lets users star repositories to bookmark them for later reference and to show appreciation. The star count is a widely used community signal of a repository's popularity. Users can view all their starred repositories at github.com/stars and organize them with lists."},
{"id":3340,"topic":"git-github","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of a GitHub Actions 'environment' deployment URL?","options":["The URL used to authenticate the GITHUB_TOKEN with the environment","A URL that GitHub displays on the PR and in the Actions UI linking to the deployed application for that environment, enabling quick visual verification","The URL GitHub uses to send webhook notifications for environment events","The endpoint URL of the GitHub Environments REST API"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When a job references a deployment environment and sets a 'url:' under the 'environment:' key (or via the Deployments API), GitHub displays this URL as a link in the PR and deployment history. Clicking it takes users directly to the deployed application (e.g., a staging or production URL), making deployment verification quick and accessible."},
]);
